### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?

The CSS side of the VT opt-in is an at-rule that looks like the following:

```css
@view-transition {
  navigation: auto;
}
```

But we need a good script hook so authors can convey the opt-in on a per navigation basis. Right now it requires hacky script (on top of the above CSS) like this:

```js
navigation.addEventListener("navigate", async e => {
  if (!shouldTransition(e)) {
     const sheet = new CSSStyleSheet();
     // none means don’t transition.
     sheet.replaceSync("@view-transition { navigation: none }");
     // Remember to remove it in case the Document goes into BFCache.
     document.adoptedStyleSheets.push(sheet);  
  }
});
```

It’s also bad because when the CSS is read to decide the opt-in for a navigation is unclear, so authors can’t know if the change made in `navigate` is guaranteed to apply. Could we do something like the following:

- Right before the `navigate` event is dispatched, the browser computes the CSS at-rule.
- The `navigate` event has an attribute with an initial value based on the compute value from CSS. But authors can overwrite it with the opt-in for that particular navigation.
- The final ViewTransition opt-in is based on the value of the `navigate` event post dispatch.

So authors don't have to update CSS for state which needs to apply to a particular navigation. The code would look something like:

```js
navigation.addEventListener("navigate", async e => {
  e.crossDocumentViewTransition = shouldTransition(e);
});
```

I was originally hoping that we can eliminate the CSS opt-in as a requirement, if all the decision making is in script then setting `crossDocumentViewTransition` is sufficient. But it becomes an issue for “browser UI” navigations. We don’t want to block committing them on dispatching an event to the old Document. That requires a declarative opt-in. So the requirement would be that the author has to opt-in to VT in CSS *before* dispatch of `navigate`. If yes, then whether VT happens for the navigation is based on `navigate.crossDocumentViewTransition`.

If the overall idea sounds reasonable then one detail is whether `crossDocumentViewTransition` is a boolean or a struct. The CSS at-rule will have more fields that also need to be in the JS API. Struct is nicer for that.

With this API in-place, I would also be ok to defer on https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/9702 for now. #9702 is for use-cases where information known after a navigation commit (like post redirect URL) requires recomputing the VT decision. We can add that event once we see concrete use-cases.

